apt 2.7.14 cmake/methods: undef bionic LFS aliases before seccomp ALLOW list

methods/aptmethod.h builds its seccomp allowlist with SCMP_SYS(what) for
each syscall name.  The ALLOW(what) macro passes `what` through to
SCMP_SYS, and because `what` is not an operand of #/## inside ALLOW, the
argument is fully macro-expanded *before* it reaches SCMP_SYS.

On bionic/musl-style libc headers (OHOS), large-file aliases are defined
as object-like macros:

  /usr/include/sys/stat.h:    #define fstatat64 fstatat
  /usr/include/sys/resource.h:#define prlimit64 prlimit

so ALLOW(fstatat64) becomes SCMP_SYS(fstatat) -> __SNR_fstatat, and
ALLOW(prlimit64) becomes SCMP_SYS(prlimit) -> __SNR_prlimit.  On aarch64
those syscall names do not exist (the arch uses newfstatat and prlimit64,
and the OHOS libseccomp seccomp-syscalls.h only defines __SNR_fstatat64
and __SNR_prlimit64), so the build fails with:

  methods/aptmethod.h:229:7: error: use of undeclared identifier '__SNR_fstatat'
  methods/aptmethod.h:294:7: error: use of undeclared identifier '__SNR_prlimit'

glibc does not alias these names, which is why upstream builds on Linux.

Fix by undefining the two LFS aliases right before the ALLOW list so the
64-bit syscall names survive to SCMP_SYS() and map to the arch-correct
__SNR_fstatat64 / __SNR_prlimit64.  No other use of fstatat64/prlimit64
exists in the file.
--- a/methods/aptmethod.h
+++ b/methods/aptmethod.h
@@ -189,6 +189,12 @@
    if ((rc = seccomp_rule_add(ctx, SCMP_ACT_ALLOW, SCMP_SYS(what), 0))) \
       return _error->FatalE("HttpMethod::Configuration", "Cannot allow %s: %s", #what, strerror(-rc));
 
+      /* bionic-style LFS aliases (fstatat64->fstatat, prlimit64->prlimit)
+       * would be expanded by the ALLOW() macro before reaching SCMP_SYS,
+       * producing arch-invalid __SNR_fstatat/__SNR_prlimit on aarch64. */
+#undef fstatat64
+#undef prlimit64
+
       for (auto &custom : _config->FindVector("APT::Sandbox::Seccomp::Trap"))
       {
 	 if ((rc = seccomp_rule_add(ctx, SCMP_ACT_TRAP, seccomp_syscall_resolve_name(custom.c_str()), 0)))

